 The engine itself enforces one server per pool, replacing the advisory pid-file convention the launcher skill used to keep. On boot the server reads `runs/server.pid` from the pool directory: if that pid belongs to a live process, it exits non-zero with a message naming the live pid, its port (from the fleet registry when known), and the pool directory; if the pid is stale or the file is absent, it writes its own pid and continues. Liveness is a signal-0 probe. There is no `--force` escape hatch — a live lock always means "use the running console or kill it".
 
 ## Acceptance criteria
 
-- [ ] Booting a second server against a pool with a live server exits non-zero, naming the live pid, its port when known, and the pool directory
-- [ ] Booting against a stale pid file succeeds and overwrites it with the new server's pid
-- [ ] The pid file is written by the server process itself, on every successful boot — no launcher involvement
-- [ ] No `--force` flag or equivalent override exists
-- [ ] Server-factory test seam: live-pid refusal, stale-pid takeover, and self-written pid each covered against temp pool directories, using real pids
-- [ ] Full test suite, typecheck, and build clean
